WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:05.976
MCCORMICK: I was 18 and I was hunting for the first
time on my own.

00:00:06.894 --> 00:00:11.846
It was quiet and misty, and the air — it's crisp.

00:00:11.846 --> 00:00:15.689
The tiny, yellow birch leaves are rustling.

00:00:15.937 --> 00:00:20.427
Suddenly out of the brush steps that bull
moose.

00:00:20.427 --> 00:00:24.270
That's when I took a few deep breaths and took the shot.&nbsp;

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.755
I remember just thanking our creator for the
gift of the meat

00:00:29.755 --> 00:00:32.567
and just, feeling like 

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:35.247
this is something that has given its life

00:00:35.247 --> 00:00:37.624
so that I can live, so that my family can live.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:41.520
For as long as they’ve lived on the shores of Lake Superior,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.071
the Ojibwe or Anishinaabe people have revered
and depended on the moose.

00:00:47.248 --> 00:00:49.638
GAWBOY: Game pieces were carved from the antlers,

00:00:49.638 --> 00:00:52.018
rattles were made from the hooves,

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:55.743
warm stockings were made of the hocks of the moose, 

00:00:55.743 --> 00:00:59.716
moose hair embroidery made beautiful clothing.

00:00:59.716 --> 00:01:02.109
The relationship to the moose is almost mythic. 

00:01:02.263 --> 00:01:06.210
But a few years ago, something strange started
happening.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.877
The moose began to disappear from the landscape.

00:01:08.877 --> 00:01:12.885
"Minnesota’s moose population fell for the 
fourteenth straight year."

00:01:12.885 --> 00:01:14.048
"An alarming decline." 

00:01:14.048 --> 00:01:17.882
"A growing mystery that has scientists baffled
and on the hunt for answers."

00:01:17.882 --> 00:01:21.611
"The moose of Minnesota are dying, and
no one knows why."

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.859
TIBBETTS: The Ojibwe people have lived along 
these banks forever.

00:01:31.859 --> 00:01:37.948
MCCORMICK: It’s just a really unique place that we
call home: the most north eastern point of

00:01:37.948 --> 00:01:42.028
Minnesota, bordered by Canada and also by
Lake Superior.

00:01:42.028 --> 00:01:44.658
This area has a relatively harsh climate.

00:01:44.658 --> 00:01:50.352
About mid-October to about early May, we typically
have snow on the ground.

00:01:50.352 --> 00:01:53.671
MOORE: It's a difficult place to live and so  

00:01:53.671 --> 00:01:57.824
subsisting off of the species that are present here
really requires some effort.

00:01:57.824 --> 00:02:02.236
MCCORMICK: My family has always harvested deer, moose.  

00:02:02.236 --> 00:02:06.279
It means something to us, it's not a sportsman
type of a hunt.

00:02:06.279 --> 00:02:12.267
It's not a "let's find out who has the biggest
set of antlers," you know.

00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:14.422
It's just a very special thing. 

00:02:14.422 --> 00:02:21.842
TIBBETTS: This last year, I went around and delivered
these baskets of moose meat to my brother,

00:02:21.842 --> 00:02:25.497
my niece, my sister.

00:02:25.497 --> 00:02:28.721
When you’re able to provide for your family yourself,

00:02:28.721 --> 00:02:34.535
I mean, that’s what we’re really put here—I think—to do.
You know, to take care of each other.

00:02:34.535 --> 00:02:38.040
It's part of the reason why we
care so much about our moose population is

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.547
because we want to continue to subsistence
hunt for future generations.

00:02:42.547 --> 00:02:46.957
But scientists were worried that hunting 
might be part of the problem.

00:02:46.957 --> 00:02:52.654
MOORE: It was our belief that human hunting was
the primary cause really affecting mortality in moose.

00:02:52.654 --> 00:02:54.809
That hypothesis made sense. 

00:02:54.809 --> 00:03:02.002
White settlers and their descendants had hunted the states native
elk and caribou out of existence.

00:03:02.002 --> 00:03:06.850
MOORE: So when we saw this decline, 
it was a very immediate decision that we

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:10.664
need to understand what's going on 
and if that decline can be reversed.

00:03:10.745 --> 00:03:15.704
To figure out what was happening, Seth and
his team started going out in helicopters.

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:19.749
When they spotted a moose, they’d
give it a tranquilizer.

00:03:19.749 --> 00:03:24.410
Then, hike to the moose, and place 
a GPS collar around its neck.

00:03:27.429 --> 00:03:31.854
After a quick shot to reverse the tranquilizer
the moose would be on its way. 

00:03:34.144 --> 00:03:38.985
MOORE: And when the moose stopped moving 
for a period greater than six hours

00:03:38.985 --> 00:03:42.746
essentially it sends a mortality signal to the satellite

00:03:42.746 --> 00:03:45.531
and then the satellite sends a text to my phone.

00:03:45.531 --> 00:03:48.536
And when we get there, typically we'll find a dead moose.

00:03:48.536 --> 00:03:51.109
We cut open the moose and take samples

00:03:51.109 --> 00:03:56.977
from every single organ system in the moose
and we send those off to a diagnostic lab.

00:03:56.977 --> 00:04:01.629
And the majority of the time, they can tell
us what was wrong with that moose.

00:04:01.629 --> 00:04:07.297
To understand these results, there’s something
else you need to know about this part of the world:

00:04:07.297 --> 00:04:10.104
MCCORMICK: Our winters have changed.

00:04:10.104 --> 00:04:17.101
Snow drifts as tall as the doorway—the last
time that happened in my lifetime was 1992.

00:04:17.101 --> 00:04:25.089
MOORE: Our average snow depth in early spring has
declined by about 70 percent and our temperatures

00:04:25.089 --> 00:04:29.419
have increased by about five degrees Fahrenheit
on average as well.

00:04:29.419 --> 00:04:35.107
The biggest moose killer that Seth and his
team discovered was the brainworm.

00:04:35.107 --> 00:04:40.800
It's a parasitethat lives in the brains 
of white-tailed deer, but doesn’t harm them.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.620
Typically, deer and moose don't share
the same habitat. 

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:51.213
With their lighter coats and shorter legs,
deer tend to thrive in places with warmer winters.

00:04:51.213 --> 00:04:56.692
But in the last 30 years, rising winter temperatures
have lured white-tailed deer

00:04:56.692 --> 00:05:01.085
from the southern part of Minnesota
into the state's northern reaches.

00:05:01.085 --> 00:05:03.806
Right into the moose's habitat. 

00:05:03.806 --> 00:05:06.565
And the deer bring the brainworm with them.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:12.325
Its eggs hatch in the deer’s feces, then slugs 
eat the newly hatched larvae,

00:05:12.325 --> 00:05:14.470
and climb up onto plants.

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:18.602
Moose eat those plants, and the slugs, and the worms

00:05:18.602 --> 00:05:20.756
which end up in their brains. 

00:05:20.756 --> 00:05:23.784
It tends to tunnel around looking for 
the right habitat for it

00:05:23.784 --> 00:05:25.020
which is a deer's brain.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.160
And it's those tunnels that caused the neurological
damage  

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.821
They keep their head tilted and they walk
in circles and 

00:05:30.821 --> 00:05:33.409
eventually they just starve to death. 

00:05:33.409 --> 00:05:38.189
When warmer winters bring white-tailed deer
further north, that causes another problem

00:05:38.189 --> 00:05:40.249
for the moose: wolves.

00:05:40.249 --> 00:05:44.564
MOORE: The wolf population in northern Minnesota
has increased dramatically.

00:05:44.564 --> 00:05:48.502
Like most predators, wolves follow their main
food source.

00:05:48.502 --> 00:05:50.816
MOORE: When the moose are giving birth,  

00:05:50.816 --> 00:05:54.650
moose calves are actually the easiest thing
on the landscape to eat. 

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:58.772
MOORE: We're losing 70 or 80 percent, each year,
within the first two weeks of life. 

00:05:58.772 --> 00:06:04.382
MOORE: And that's just too high a rate of mortality
for the population to replace itself.

00:06:05.495 --> 00:06:12.775
Warmer winters amplify a third major threat
to the moose: infestations by winter ticks.

00:06:12.775 --> 00:06:17.775
These parasites spend the entire winter attached
to the skin of the moose.

00:06:17.775 --> 00:06:21.712
Then, in the early spring, they drop off to
lay their eggs.

00:06:21.712 --> 00:06:25.187
MOORE: If they drop off between, say, 
February and April, 

00:06:25.187 --> 00:06:29.129
 if there is is deep snow on the ground, 
it essentially kills them off.

00:06:29.129 --> 00:06:33.839
Enough ticks survive to jump on the moose
and start the cycle again, but enough die

00:06:33.839 --> 00:06:35.795
so that the moose aren’t overwhelmed.

00:06:35.795 --> 00:06:38.646
But in recent decades, that deep spring snow

00:06:38.646 --> 00:06:41.935
hasn’t been there to kill off a portion of the ticks.

00:06:41.935 --> 00:06:43.808
 MOORE: So the winter ticks are hitting the ground, 

00:06:43.808 --> 00:06:45.389
they're all surviving.

00:06:45.389 --> 00:06:46.754
MOORE: Their eggs are surviving.

00:06:46.754 --> 00:06:51.968
When winter comes around, all those surviving
ticks attach themselves to the moose.

00:06:51.968 --> 00:06:55.105
MOORE: Sometimes you'll see a moose with a hundred
thousand ticks on it.

00:06:55.105 --> 00:07:00.680
MOORE: They aren't eating well because the ticks
are so uncomfortable they’re constantly

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:02.620
just trying to scrape them off of their body 

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.441
and they end up starving to death or
becoming anemic. 

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:13.038
MOORE: They’re called ghost moose because
they’re missing 60 to 70 percent of their hair.

00:07:13.038 --> 00:07:14.311
MOORE: It's just bare skin. 

00:07:14.311 --> 00:07:16.826
MOORE: I mean, it’s horrifying looking.

00:07:19.351 --> 00:07:23.462
MOORE: The moose population is declining directly as
a consequence of climate change.

00:07:23.462 --> 00:07:27.866
MOORE: There's just no reason to stop the subsistence hunt. 

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:35.585
People always talk about Indians living 
right on the edge of starvation

00:07:35.585 --> 00:07:37.405
or on the edge of survival. 

00:07:37.405 --> 00:07:41.791
GAWBOY: But not only did they provide themselves
with this great diet,

00:07:41.791 --> 00:07:44.841
they had time to decorate even down to little babies. 

00:07:44.841 --> 00:07:48.052
GAWBOY: Little babies' outfits were beautifully decorated. 

00:07:48.052 --> 00:07:50.689
I mean, who knows that these days? 

00:07:50.689 --> 00:07:54.166
GAWBOY: Every time I think about the Ojibwe people through time,

00:07:54.166 --> 00:08:00.803
I just kind of shake my head and I thought,
why don't people know this? 

00:08:04.388 --> 00:08:10.000
Four hundred years ago, the Ojibwe lands stretched
for nearly 2,000 square miles,

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:14.342
from Lake Ontario all the way to the Northern Great Plains.

00:08:14.342 --> 00:08:18.042
It’s where they hunted, fished, and harvested.

00:08:18.042 --> 00:08:23.126
Where they danced, raised their children,
and buried their loved ones.

00:08:23.126 --> 00:08:25.879
European settlers wanted that land.

00:08:25.879 --> 00:08:26.846
To farm.

00:08:26.846 --> 00:08:27.846
To log.

00:08:27.846 --> 00:08:28.793
To mine.

00:08:28.793 --> 00:08:31.063
To drill for oil and gas.

00:08:31.063 --> 00:08:36.599
In a few hundred years, the descendents of
those settlers helped build an economy around

00:08:36.599 --> 00:08:40.579
extracting, refining, and burning that oil and gas,

00:08:40.579 --> 00:08:42.764
pushing up global temperatures,

00:08:42.764 --> 00:08:49.926
altering ecosystems, and changing the life
cycles of species large and small.

00:08:49.926 --> 00:08:54.527
Today, the Ojibwe homelands are a fraction
of what they once were.

00:08:54.527 --> 00:08:56.123
But their culture has endured.

00:08:56.123 --> 00:09:00.442
TIBBETTS: The resiliency of native peoples is—
it can't be questioned, you know?

00:09:00.442 --> 00:09:01.897
We're still here. 

00:09:01.897 --> 00:09:07.430
We've always just felt more connected to 
our environment. How we treat the Earth.

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:13.412
You know, I always wondered, growing up, if the people 
would've taken the time to learn more about

00:09:13.412 --> 00:09:14.809
the Anishinaabe. 

00:09:14.809 --> 00:09:17.734
You know, what would this country look like? 

00:09:17.734 --> 00:09:19.764
I think it would be a lot stronger.

